L74: This article provides reference information for the configuration options available in the `dependabot.yml` file. Use these options to customize how Dependabot monitors package ecosystems, schedules updates, and creates pull requests. For an overview of the `dependabot.yml` file and how it works, see cite27†About the dependabot.yml file .
L75: All options marked with a icon also change how Dependabot creates pull requests for security updates, except where `target-branch` is used.
L190:   * All pull requests for version updates are created with the chosen assignees.
L191:   * All pull requests for security updates are created with the chosen assignees, unless `target-branch` defines updates to a non-default branch.
L192: 
L193: Assignees must have write access to the repository. For organization-owned repositories, organization members with read access are also valid assignees.
L194: ## cite6†`commit-message` L195: 
L196: Define the format for commit messages. Since the titles of pull requests are written based on commit messages, this setting also impacts the titles of pull requests. For examples, see cite35†Customizing Dependabot pull requests to fit your processes .
L197: 
L198: Dependabot default behavior:
L199: 
L200:   * Commit messages follow similar patterns to those detected in the repository.
L201: 
L202: When `commit-message` is defined:
L210: Tip
L211: 
L212: When pull requests are raised for grouped updates, the branch name and pull request title are defined by the group `IDENTIFIER`, see cite10†`groups` .
L230: ## cite7†`cooldown` L231: 
L232: Defines a cooldown period for dependency updates, allowing updates to be delayed for a configurable number of days. The `cooldown` option is only available for version updates, not security updates.
L233: 
L234: This feature enables users to customize how often Dependabot generates new version updates, offering greater control over update frequency. For examples, see cite39†Optimizing the creation of pull requests for Dependabot version updates .
L235: 
L236: Dependabot default behavior:
L317: ## cite10†`groups` L318: 
L319: Define rules to create one or more sets of dependencies managed by a package manager, to group updates into fewer, targeted pull requests. For examples, see cite42†Optimizing the creation of pull requests for Dependabot version updates .
L320: 
L321: Dependabot default behavior:
L322: 
L323:   * Open a single pull request for each dependency that needs to be updated to a newer version for version updates and for security updates.
L324: 
L325: When `groups` is used to define rules:
L326:   * All updates for dependencies that match a rule are combined in a single pull request.
L327:   * If a dependency matches more than one rule, it's included in the first group that it matches.
L328:   * Any outdated dependencies that do not match a rule are updated in individual pull requests.
L329: Parameters  | Purpose
L330: --- | ---
L346: ### cite44†`group-by` (`groups`) L347: 
L348: Use `groups.<group-name>.group-by` to specify how Dependabot should group updates across multiple directories in a monorepo.
L349: 
L350:   * Type: String
L351:   * Accepted values: `dependency-name`
L352:   * Applies to: Configurations with multiple directories specified
L353: 
L354: When set to `dependency-name`, Dependabot will create a single pull request for each dependency update across all specified directories, rather than separate pull requests per directory.
L355: 
L356: Limitations of cross-directory grouping
L437: ## cite13†`labels` L438: 
L439: Specify your own labels for all pull requests raised for a package manager. For examples, see cite35†Customizing Dependabot pull requests to fit your processes .
L440: 
L441: Dependabot default behavior:
L442:   * All pull requests have a `dependencies` label.
L443:   * If you define more than one package manager, an additional label for the ecosystem or language is added to each pull request. For example: `java` for Gradle updates and `submodules` for git submodule updates.
L444:   * If semantic version (SemVer) labels are present in the repository, they will be applied automatically to indicate the type of version update (`major`, `minor`, or `patch`).
L452: Setting this option will also affect pull requests for security updates to the manifest files of this package manager, unless you use `target-branch` to check for version updates on a non-default branch.
L453: ## cite14†`milestone` L454: 
L455: Associate all pull requests raised for a package manager with a milestone. For examples, see cite35†Customizing Dependabot pull requests to fit your processes .
L456: 
L457: Dependabot default behavior:
L458: 
L459:   * No milestones are used.
L460: 
L461: When `milestone` is defined:
L462: 
L463:   * All pull requests for the package manager are added to the milestone.
L464: 
L465: Supported value: the numeric identifier of a milestone.
L466: 
L467: Tip
L468: If you view a milestone, the final part of the page URL, after `milestone`, is the identifier. For example: `https://github.com/<org>/<repo>/milestone/3`, see cite55†Viewing your milestone's progress .
L469: ## cite15†`multi-ecosystem-groups` L470: 
L471: Define groups that span multiple package ecosystems to get a single Dependabot pull request that updates all supported package ecosystems. This approach helps reduce the number of Dependabot pull requests you receive and streamlines your dependency update workflow.
L472: 
L473: Dependabot default behavior:
L474: 
L475:   * Create separate pull requests for each package ecosystem that has dependency updates.
L476: 
L477: When `multi-ecosystem-groups` is used:
L480: ### cite56†`multi-ecosystem-group` L481: 
L482: Assign individual package ecosystems to a multi-ecosystem group using the `multi-ecosystem-group` parameter in your `updates` configuration.
L483: 
L484: Important
L485: 
L486: Multi-ecosystem updates require specific configuration patterns and have unique parameter merging behavior. For complete setup instructions, configuration examples, and detailed parameter reference, see cite57†Configuring multi-ecosystem updates for Dependabot .
L487: YAML
L488: 
L489:     # Basic `dependabot.yml` file defining a multi-ecosystem-group
L490:     version: 2
L491: 
L492:     multi-ecosystem-groups:
L493:       infrastructure:
